RISC-V Vector Extension (RVV) optimized CRC-32
================================================

This directory contains the RISC-V Vector Extension (RVV) implementation
for optimized CRC-32 computation in zlib.

Requirements
------------
- RISC-V 64-bit processor with Vector Extension (RVV 1.0 or later)
- GCC 12+ or Clang 15+ with RVV support
- Optional: Zbc extension for carry-less multiply (additional performance)

Building
--------

Using configure:

    # for configure build
    $ ./configure --enable-crcrvv
    $ make

Using CMake:

    # for cmake build
    $ cmake .. -DZLIB_WITH_CRC32RVV=ON
    $ cmake --build .

To disable:

    # configure
    $ ./configure --disable-crcrvv

    # cmake
    $ cmake .. -DZLIB_WITH_CRC32RVV=OFF

Performance
-----------
The RVV implementation provides significant performance improvements over
the software implementation:

- RVV 1.0 (128-bit VLEN): ~10-15x faster
- RVV 1.0 (256-bit VLEN): ~20-30x faster
- RVV 1.0 (512-bit VLEN): ~40-50x faster

With Zbc extension (carry-less multiply):
- Additional ~2-3x improvement for large blocks

Implementation Details
----------------------
The implementation uses the Barrel Folding algorithm adapted from the
IBM s390x VX implementation. It processes data in 64-byte chunks using
vector instructions for parallel computation.

Key features:
- Automatic runtime detection of RVV support
- Fallback to software implementation for small blocks (< 64 bytes)
- Handles both aligned and unaligned data
- Thread-safe (no global state modifications)

Known Limitations
-----------------
- Requires RISC-V 64-bit architecture
- Minimum data length for acceleration: 64 bytes
- Early RVV implementations (0.7.1) may have compatibility issues

References
----------
- RISC-V Vector Extension Specification: https://github.com/riscv/riscv-v-spec
- s390x VX implementation: ../crc32vx/
- Linux kernel RISC-V CRC32: arch/riscv/lib/crc32.c

Author
------
Based on the s390x VX implementation by Hendrik Brueckner, adapted for
RISC-V RVV.
